<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST10309"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Lostdog<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Lostdog<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Thursday, March 03, 2005 - 3:04 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Speaking of username etymology: <BR> <BR>HAYTRADER, you posted as early as February 8th. <BR> <BR>I’m thinking your handle might have cost the puzzle loving monster at least one good night’s sleep. <BR> <BR>I hate Rader. How about you? <BR> <BR>Do you operate an alfalfa exchange? <BR> <BR>CattleChowAgent, BaleBroker, FeedStoreClerk. You could have used any of those.   <BR> <BR>Or is it more than that Hay?... HAY? How much do you see? <BR> <BR>Is it a good time to buy Martha Stewart Living OmniMedia &#40;MSO&#41;? Should I sell King of Pop Inc. &#40;MJP&#41;? Is the Mike Tyson chicken broaster going to be the next big thing? <BR> <BR>We’re all friends here. Tell us what you know. <BR> <BR>LolDog <BR> <BR> <BR>Huff, <BR> <BR>I’m definitely going to use those seat protectors in public restrooms from here on out. <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 10309-->
<!--Post: 10314--><!--Time: 1109894080-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST10314"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Lostdog<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Lostdog<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Thursday, March 03, 2005 - 3:54 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Lil, <BR> <BR>You crack me up big time   a  n  d  you did stir my manhood just a little bit with your hilarious “stone of shame post” - LQTM &#40;laughing quietly to myself&#41; – my beautiful wife is right here and her sense of humor has… limits. <BR> <BR> <BR> <BR>All:  <BR> <BR>Seriously, let us laugh to keep from crying. There is, after all, nothing funny about BTK. Just like the dark humor of cops and EMTs who deal with suffering and death and lowlifes all day every day, our group obsession needed a release. <BR> <BR>With eyelids pinned back, we discussed a most abysmal and tragic subject. We were rightly forbidden to change the subject – FOCUS PEOPLE, STAY ON TOPIC!  <BR> <BR>We made friends, tenuously, and wouldn’t dare share the details that made us human. <BR> <BR>We logged off wondering if the vile beast was reading our words at the same moment we checked our deadbolts and listened for the breath of our sleeping babies. Self indulgence took a nasty hold.  <BR> <BR>“I know it’s just my dusky silhouette in the mirror – but that’s how it looked when it started. Why did the porch light have to burn out tonight? What just made Simba’s ears stand up?” <BR> <BR>Sleep jacking nightmares left us wondering if we’d had a vision. A vision that would pit left brain versus right -- a vision that would have to wait to be filtered and posted and scrutinized. <BR>  <BR>F&#42;#king murderers! I’ve had an aching gutful. Couldn’t sleep because of Zodiac in my backyard, couldn’t take a hike while the Trailside Killer was at work, sweated out the unbearable sticky, airless L.A. summer of Richard Ramirez. <BR> <BR>Laughter is an overdue therapy for our functionally dysfunctional relationship. The end of the hunt is bittersweet. Praise God that this monster is done. So many mysteries will melt in to fact. Maddening ironies will be exposed -- but --  <BR> <BR>Nancy won’t be having the grandkids over for Easter Brunch.  <BR> <BR>Vicki won’t be raising her fingertips to her cheeks in delight as she receives crayon and glue Jesus art from her Sunday children. <BR> <BR>Josie won’t be carried across a threshold in a noisy white gown while Joe and Julie go home to cry. <BR> <BR>No. <BR> <BR>Instead they’re cradled in the grand, swaying oaks of their ancestors. Sun dappled spirits in a heavenly grove. I want to believe they hear our voices, prayers and laughter ringing off the leaves… and I want to believe they cheered us on and laughed with us when it felt so good, at last, to laugh. <BR> <BR> <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 10314-->
<!--Post: 10317--><!--Time: 1109895471-->
